<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Dal nAraidi</h1><p class="st-meta">450 CE – 1100 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Leading kingdom of the Cruthin in south Antrim, whose king Congal Cáech fought for the high kingship and fell at Mag Rath in 637.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Dál nAraidi were the leading dynasty of the Cruthin, a people of east <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-ireland">Ulster</a> distinguished in early Irish sources from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ulaid">Ulaid</a> proper. By the 6th century they held south and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">County Antrim</a>, with their royal centre in Mag Line near the town of Antrim, and they competed with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dal-fiatach">Dal Fiatach</a> for the kingship of the Kingdom of Ulaid.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiachnae-mac-baetain">Fiachnae mac Báetáin</a>, who died in 626, became king of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ulaid">Ulaid</a> and is said to have campaigned in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congal-caech">Congal Cáech</a>, king of the Ulaid and briefly claimant to the kingship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-of-tara">Tara</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> but was defeated and killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domnall-mac-aedo">Domnall mac Áedo</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-moira">Mag Rath</a> in 637. St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-comgall">Comgall</a>, founder of the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangor-abbey">Bangor</a>, belonged to this people.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-moira">Mag Rath</a> their power declined, though their kings still held the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ulaid">Ulaid</a> kingship at times until the 10th century. Pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raiders, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dal-fiatach">Dal Fiatach</a> reduced them, and by the 12th century their lands passed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Norman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-neill-dynasty">Ua Néill</a> rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dal-naraidi.jpg" alt="Dal nAraidi" width="320" height="260"><figcaption>Image: Mabuska · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UlsterDioceseKingdoms.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>450 CE – 1100 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fiachnae mac Báetáin, Congal Cáech</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Irish, later Middle Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United Kingdom</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dál nAraidi, Cruthin</dd></dl></aside></div>

